(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/id "adr-2609106000-the-kir-block-shape-is-an-ipld-schema-and-what-it-cannot-pin")
  (:adr/topic "kir-block-shape-is-an-ipld-schema")
  (:adr/title "The KIR block shape is an IPLD Schema, two things it cannot pin, and four surface families the convergence claim did not cover")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-10")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/osaho" "kotoba-lang/amu" "kotoba-lang/io-ipld"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609076000-kir-is-an-ipld-codec-schema-and-adl"
                "adr-2609081400-a-grammar-is-a-surface-not-a-language"
                "adr-2609092600-app-plane-identity-naming-location-kind"))
  (:adr/body
  "## Context

ADR-2609081400 measured an external design proposal (Unison x Lisp x IPLD) against
what Kotoba already had, and left a table with two rows unfinished and one row
untested. This ADR records closing two of them, and names a red on osaho main that
the work walked into.

The two addressed here:

- ADR-2609076000 decision 2 -- the KIR block shape is an explicit IPLD Schema,
  written in ipld.schema-dsl and validated as Schema DMT by
  ipld.schema/compile-schema. It was the one decision in that ADR with nothing on
  disk.
- ADR-2609081400's stated limit -- surface convergence was discharged for three
  pairs, and destructuring, protocol dispatch, match and the document forms were
  named as unmeasured.

## Decision 1: the schema is a resource, and it pins shape only

`osaho/resources/kotoba/kir/definition-identity.ipldsch` describes the DAG-CBOR
block a DefCID addresses -- the output of `normalize` over `identity-payload`,
which is what `cbor/encode` is handed.

It is a RESOURCE, not a source dependency. ADR-2609076000 decision 1 landed
io-ipld in osaho test-only on purpose, because promoting it to `:deps` is the
payload v3 decision. Compiling a schema costs no byte of any block, so a schema
belongs on that side of the line. No CID moved.

What is pinned, and is therefore no longer prose: the closed twelve-tag
vocabulary (one per branch of `normalize`), the arity of each variant, the
data-model kind of each body, the map entry shape, and the named-reference
closure tying the four collection bodies back to Node.

## Decision 2: two things IPLD Schema cannot express here, named in the file

Measured against io-ipld 973e26a5.

**The positional discriminator.** A node is a list whose element 0 is the tag.
Every union strategy ipld.schema supports -- keyed, kinded, envelope, inline,
stringprefix, bytesprefix -- discriminates on a map key, a data-model kind, or a
string or byte prefix. None reads a discriminant out of list position 0, and a
kinded union over these twelve is refused at compile time as an ambiguous table
because all twelve are the list kind. So Node is declared as a bare list and the
dispatch is the checker's.

**The tag bound to its variant.** `tag Tag` admits all twelve members in every
variant, so a nil-tagged two-element node unifies against IntNode. There is no
singleton string type to bind one member. The checker asserts the equality the
enum cannot.

The second is ASSERTED rather than described:
`the-schema-alone-cannot-tell-a-nil-node-from-an-int-node` requires that
unification to SUCCEED. If ipld.schema ever gains a singleton type, that
assertion fails -- which is how a check that has become redundant gets
reconsidered deliberately, instead of being deleted after someone notices nothing
goes red.

## Decision 3: the shape verdict refuses to answer checkedness

ADR-2609076000 decision 4 said a block that validates against the schema and then
fails admission must not report what one that passes both reports. That is now in
code, not only in prose: the verdict carries `:checked :not-answered`, and a test
fails if it ever grows a checkedness key. The schema is not an admission gate and
does not pretend to be one.

## Decision 4: the convergence corpus covers four families, and the core spelling
must be the DOCUMENTED desugar target

`amu/test/kotoba/compiler/surface_convergence_portable_test.cljc` pairs each of
four constructs with its canonical-core spelling and asserts equal DefCIDs, with
a negative control per case. It runs on the file-free seam `definition-cids` uses
(sema/analyze then di/definitions), so it is exercised on nbb as well as the JVM
rather than shelling out to a built CLI.

The finding worth carrying is not that they converge. It is that the FIRST
attempt reported divergence for two of the four, and both times the probe was
wrong: `nth` with a default is a different call from the pair lookup
`:nested-destructuring` names, and an `if` on the scrutinee skips the let that
`:match`'s single-evaluation requires. Both spellings are semantically equivalent
to their sugar and neither is what the grammar says the sugar becomes.

Reported the other way round, that would have entered the record as a language
defect that does not exist. So: **a case that reports divergence is a claim about
the probe until the core spelling is checked against lang/guest-grammar.edn**, and
the test's own failure message says so to whoever reads the red.

A second shape is recorded there: the protocol case's control changes a method
body, so the record CONSTRUCTOR correctly does not move. The assertion is that
the control moves at least one definition, not every one. Asserting every one
would be false for the right reason, and would get fixed by weakening the case.

## What was found and NOT fixed: osaho main is red

`kotoba.kir-bool-parameter-test/the-native-gate-still-refuses-what-it-always-refuses`
fails on an unmodified worktree at osaho origin/main 7a6a27b: the native
admission gate returns true where the test requires false. It is not caused by
the schema work -- a clean detached worktree reproduces it alone -- and it was
already there when the schema branch merged onto main. Named here so it is a
finding with a date rather than a surprise for the next reader.

## Reproduce

The schema, in an osaho worktree at origin/main (NOT the shared checkout):

    git -C orgs/kotoba-lang/osaho worktree add --detach <dir> origin/main
    cd <dir> && clojure -M:test -n kotoba.kir.definition-identity-schema-test

It prints a SCANNED line carrying the block and node counts. To confirm it
discriminates rather than merely passing, change IntNode's body from String to
Int in the .ipldsch and run it again: the frozen vectors go red, because
integers are carried as decimal text and that is what keeps :clj and :cljs on
the same bytes.

The convergence corpus, in an amu worktree at origin/main:

    git -C orgs/kotoba-lang/amu worktree add --detach <dir> origin/main
    ln -s <shared amu>/node_modules <dir>/node_modules
    cd <dir> && npm run test-nbb-portable-tests

It prints one CONVERGED line per family. To confirm both halves discriminate,
replace one core spelling with a semantically equivalent but undocumented one
(the agreement half fails), then make one control equal to its sugar (the
discrimination half fails, naming itself).

Do not run either in the shared checkout. It is behind origin/main, and a pure-S
spelling there returns :subset-reject, which reads as the language refusing the
form.

## What is NOT claimed

- The schema does not validate a nested node through unification. Node is a bare
  list, so a child is only checked to be a list by the schema; the recursion is
  the checker's walk.
- The schema says nothing about canonical ORDER inside a set or a map. That is
  the producer's invariant and the differential test's business.
- payload v3 is untouched. io-ipld remains test-only in osaho and every frozen
  vector still disagrees with kotoba.value.v1.
- Per-node CID remains undecided and unbuilt. ADR-2609081400's reasoning stands:
  a subterm under a binder is an open term, and the cheaper mechanism for
  node-level editing is a path into a sealed block, which ipld.selector and
  ipld.graph already provide.
- The corpus covers four families. The document forms named by ADR-2609081400
  were not measured here.
- Neither landing is a fleet gate. The schema test runs under osaho's
  `clojure -M:test`; the corpus runs under amu's portable nbb suite and its PR
  checks.")))
